How to run the Zero Trust SDN project
=====================================

Prerequisites
-------------

Use a Linux VM or host that supports Mininet and Open vSwitch. Ubuntu is the
most common choice for this project.

Install system packages:

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -y mininet openvswitch-switch python3 python3-pip git

Install Python dependencies:

    python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt

If Ryu is not available from pip on your Python version, install it from source:

    git clone https://github.com/faucetsdn/ryu.git
    cd ryu
    python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt
    python3 -m pip install .

Terminal 1: start the controller
--------------------------------

From this project folder:

    ryu-manager my_controller.py

Expected result:

    Loaded 6 Zero Trust hosts and 20 policy pairs

Keep this terminal open so you can watch ALLOW and DENY log events.

Terminal 2: start Mininet
-------------------------

From this project folder:

    sudo python3 my_custom_topology.py

Alternative Mininet command:

    sudo mn --custom my_custom_topology.py --topo custom_tree_topo --controller remote,ip=127.0.0.1,port=6653 --switch ovs,protocols=OpenFlow13 --mac

Useful Mininet cleanup command if a previous run crashed:

    sudo mn -c

Connectivity tests
------------------

Inside the Mininet CLI, run allowed tests:

    h1 ping -c 3 h2
    h1 ping -c 3 h4
    h3 ping -c 3 h4
    h4 ping -c 3 h5
    h6 ping -c 3 h5

Run denied tests:

    h3 ping -c 3 h5
    h1 ping -c 3 h5
    h2 ping -c 3 h5

The allowed tests should receive replies. The denied tests should fail and the
controller terminal should print DENY log messages.

Attack simulation examples
--------------------------

Port scan from guest host h3 against the allowed web host h4:

    h3 nmap -p 1-30 10.0.0.4

Packet-rate test from h3:

    h3 ping -f -c 100 10.0.0.4

Performance test with iperf:

    h4 iperf -s &
    h1 iperf -c 10.0.0.4 -t 10

Exit
----

Inside Mininet:

    exit

Then clean up if needed:

    sudo mn -c
